---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

import { getTechnicalAreaById } from "../../data/technicalAreas.js";

export const article = {
	title: "Large-World Coordinates",
	description:
		"Designing a coordinate system that preserves large-scale world placement without sacrificing local floating-point precision.",
	areaId: "spatial-systems",
	published: null,
	draft: true,
};

const area = getTechnicalAreaById(article.areaId);

if (!area) {
	throw new Error(
		`Unknown technical area "${article.areaId}" in large-world-coordinates.astro`,
	);
}
---

<BaseLayout title={`${article.title} | Aaron's Dev Experiments`}>
	<article class="article-page">
		<header class="article-header">
			<p class="eyebrow">
				<a href={area.href}>{area.title}</a>
			</p>

			<h1>{article.title}</h1>

			<p class="intro">
				{article.description}
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="article-content">
			<p>
				Large virtual worlds eventually run into a precision problem if
				positions are represented only as large floating-point values.
				As coordinates move farther from the origin, fewer bits remain
				available for describing small local differences, which can
				introduce instability into geometry, movement, and rendering.
			</p>

			<h2>The Coordinate Problem</h2>

			<p>
				Plane Voxel Lab originally used conventional world-space
				coordinates for many operations. That works well near the
				origin, but it creates an architectural problem once the world
				is expected to extend across a much larger range.
			</p>

			<p>
				The goal of the redesign was to preserve precise global
				placement without forcing every local operation to use large
				absolute coordinates.
			</p>

			<h2>Chunk + Local Position</h2>

			<p>
				The primary world-position representation separates position
				into two parts: an integer chunk coordinate and a bounded
				floating-point position inside that chunk.
			</p>

			<p>
				The chunk coordinate carries the large-scale location, while the
				local position remains small enough for ordinary floating-point
				arithmetic. This keeps rendering, mesh generation, and nearby
				spatial calculations working with values that remain close to
				the local origin.
			</p>

			<h2>Absolute Metric Coordinates</h2>

			<p>
				Some systems still need a stable absolute coordinate along a
				single axis. Procedural terrain sampling is one example:
				neighboring chunks must produce continuous noise even though
				each chunk performs most of its work in local coordinates.
			</p>

			<p>
				For those cases, the lab uses a separate metric-coordinate
				representation containing an integer number of whole meters and
				a fractional-meter component. This allows global scalar
				coordinates to remain stable without reconstructing a giant
				floating-point world position.
			</p>

			<h2>Camera-Relative Rendering</h2>

			<p>
				The same idea extends naturally to rendering. Rather than
				passing large absolute positions to the GPU, chunk positions are
				first expressed relative to the camera's current chunk. Local
				mesh vertices are then combined with that nearby chunk offset
				and the camera's local position.
			</p>

			<p>
				The final values used by the renderer therefore stay relatively
				small, even when the camera itself is located very far from the
				world origin.
			</p>

			<h2>What Changed</h2>

			<p>
				The refactor affected more than the camera. Density fields,
				procedural noise, heightmap sampling, terrain mesh construction,
				debug rendering, and experiment code all had to agree on the
				same coordinate model.
			</p>

			<p>
				The result is a clearer separation between global placement and
				local computation. Large distances are handled with integer
				coordinates, while floating-point values are reserved for the
				small-scale work they are best suited for.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="article-project">
			<h2>Related Project</h2>

			<a class="project-card" href="/projects/plane-voxel-lab">
				<h3>Plane Voxel Lab</h3>

				<p>
					An experimental C++ / OpenGL lab for exploring voxel terrain
					representation, surface construction, rendering, and
					editable world systems.
				</p>
			</a>
		</section>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>
